# Agent permissions — gated actions

Never do the actions below on your own. Only when the user explicitly asks in this conversation.

When work needs any of them: **stop**, tell the user what is needed and why (blast radius), and ask whether they will do it themselves or allow you to proceed. Wait for yes before acting.

## Git / GitHub (and similar)

| Forbidden unless asked | Examples |
|------------------------|----------|
| Commit | `git commit`, amend, stage-for-commit (`git add` for commit) |
| Pull request | `gh pr create`, open PR via any tool |
| Issue | `gh issue create`, Linear/GitHub issue create |
| Branch / checkout | `git checkout`, `git switch`, `git branch` create/rename/delete, create worktree |
| Stash | `git stash`, `stash push/pop/apply/drop` |
| Mutating remotes / history | `git push`, `pull`/`fetch`+merge/rebase that changes local branch tip, `reset`, `rebase`, `merge` (unless user asked), force-push |
| Other working-tree mutation via git | `git clean`, `git restore`/`checkout --` that discards or overwrites user files |

**Still allowed without ask:** read-only git/gh (`status`, `diff`, `log`, `show`, `branch -v` list, `pr view`, `issue view`, etc.). Proposing a commit message, branch name, or PR body as chat text only is fine — do not execute.

**User owns git state.** Do not stash, switch branches, or create branches to “make room” for agent work. If dirty tree or wrong branch blocks you: report it and ask the user what to do.

### PR description skill (offer, then gate)

- After meaningful work that may need a PR: ask if the user wants a PR description.
- Only if they agree / say yes, or explicitly ask: read and follow `.cursor/skills/pr-description/SKILL.md`.
- Writing a description ≠ creating a PR. Opening the PR still needs explicit allow above.

## Database

| Forbidden unless asked | Examples |
|------------------------|----------|
| Schema / migration **writes** | TypeORM entity schema edits, new/changed migration files, `db:generate` |
| Schema / data **apply** | `db:migrate`, `db:revert`, seed scripts, mutating SQL, MCP postgres writes, any prod/staging data change |

**Still allowed without ask:** read-only inspect (SELECT, describe schema, read entity/migration files).

## Production

No agent touches production. Not with a flag, not with approval, not "just this once".

- No production SSH, database credentials, or admin tokens.
- No mutating command against a production service or dataset.
- Infrastructure and schema changes are written as versioned, idempotent, reviewable recipes
  and applied to staging. A human promotes to production.
- A one-off command run by hand is knowledge that evaporates. Write the recipe instead.

## Secrets and the working tree

The most frequent real accident is not a deleted repo — it is a secret reaching a remote.

- Before any push, read `git status` and `git diff --cached`. Name the files being sent.
- Never stage a `.env`, credential file, token, key, or dump. Commit a `.example` instead and
  ignore the real one.
- If a secret has already been committed, stop and tell the user immediately. Rotating the
  secret is the fix; removing the file is not.

## How to escalate

Use a short explicit callout, e.g.:

> **Needs your decision:** [commit | PR | issue | branch/stash/checkout | DB write/migrate] — [why]. Want to do it yourself, or allow me to run: `[exact command / file scope]`?
